From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: rkvdec: Scale the RK3528's picture down by half

The RK3528's decoder writes a second picture, scaled down by an integer
ratio each way, beside the one it reconstructs and references, at
registers 198 and 199 with its ratios in register 29: a 4K stream
decoded for a 1080p display needs a quarter of the scanout bandwidth.

A client asks as for other stateless decoders that scale: S_SELECTION
with the COMPOSE target on CAPTURE, before CAPTURE buffers exist. The
ratio is the smallest power of two the variant can, 2 on the RK3528,
that fits the picture in the rectangle; G_FMT then reports the scaled
format. A buffer holds the scaled NV12 picture first, the full picture
after it, then the collocated motion vectors, so references and row
buffers keep the full size. 8-bit 4:2:0 linear pictures only, for H.264,
HEVC and VP9. The fit is judged on the stream's coded size, not the
decoder's padding of it: VP9's 2176 lines would not halve into 1080.

Half is the limit, measured on the RK3518 with video.py's scale suite,
a 3840x2160 stream asked for 1080, 540 and 270 lines: half within 65 dB
of FFmpeg's area scale for each codec; a quarter and an eighth about
10 dB, with every ratio code from 1 to 24 tried.

Register 29's ratio fields are five bits each way, as Rockchip's MPP
lays them out; nothing wrote the two-bit fields before. A TRY_FMT no
longer moves the running buffer layout.

Signed-off-by: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
